<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/living-room.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/living-room-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/living-room.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li aria-current="page">Appraisal FAQ</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Answers</p><h1>Insurance Appraisal FAQ for Washington Property Owners</h1><p class="lede">Plain answers to the questions we hear most, grouped by topic: the process, costs and timing, Washington rules and working with us.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-y" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line light" href="/talk-to-an-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><nav class="toc" aria-label="Topics"><p class="toc-t">Topics</p><ol><li><a href="#faq-1">Appraisal basics</a></li><li><a href="#faq-2">Costs and timing</a></li><li><a href="#faq-3">Washington rules</a></li><li><a href="#faq-4">Working with us</a></li></ol></nav><h2 id="faq-1">Appraisal basics</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>What is insurance appraisal?<svg class="ico chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It is a process written into most property policies for settling disagreements about the amount of a covered loss. Each side names an appraiser, the appraisers choose an umpire, and an award signed by two of the three sets the amount.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is appraisal the same as arbitration?<svg class="ico chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>They’re similar but not the same. Appraisal is narrower: it decides the amount of loss, not legal questions or coverage. It’s usually less formal and faster.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who can demand appraisal?<svg class="ico chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Either you or the insurer, as long as the policy has an appraisal clause and the disagreement is about the amount of loss.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is the award binding?<svg class="ico chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>On the amount of loss, yes, once two of the three panel members sign. Courts can set awards aside in limited circumstances, such as fraud or a panel exceeding its authority.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does appraisal decide coverage?<svg class="ico chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>No. If the insurer says something isn’t covered, that is a coverage dispute. Appraisal can value the damage, but whether it is payable may still be argued.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="faq-2">Costs and timing</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>How much does appraisal cost?<svg class="ico chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Each side pays its own appraiser and usually half the umpire’s fee. Appraiser fees may be flat, hourly or a percentage of the increase. Get any arrangement in writing before work starts.</p></div></details><details><summary>How long does it take?<svg class="ico chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Straightforward home claims often finish in six to twelve weeks after both appraisers are named. Commercial and total-loss claims take longer.</p></div></details><details><summary>When is a gap too small for appraisal?<svg class="ico chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>As a rough guide, if the difference is under about 10% of the insurer’s estimate or a few thousand dollars, a documented supplement request may be more cost-effective. Our appraisal payoff calculator can help you check.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does appraisal stop the suit-limitation deadline?<svg class="ico chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Not automatically. Washington law does not let policies shorten the time to sue to less than one year from the loss, and many homeowner policies use exactly one year. Protect that deadline separately and talk to an attorney if it’s close.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="faq-3">Washington rules</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who regulates insurers in Washington?<svg class="ico chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>The Office of the Insurance Commissioner (OIC), based in Tumwater. It licenses insurers and adjusters, enforces claims-handling rules and takes consumer complaints.</p></div></details><details><summary>What is the Insurance Fair Conduct Act?<svg class="ico chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>IFCA is a Washington law that gives policyholders a legal remedy when an insurer unreasonably denies coverage or payment of benefits. It has notice requirements before suit. It is a legal claim, separate from appraisal, so ask a Washington attorney about it.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does Washington have a FAIR plan or wind pool?<svg class="ico chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Washington does not currently operate a state FAIR plan or windstorm pool like some coastal states. Homeowners who can’t find standard coverage often turn to the surplus lines market. Lawmakers and the OIC have discussed options as wildfire risk grows.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is earthquake covered?<svg class="ico chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Not under standard homeowner policies. Earthquake coverage is sold separately or by endorsement, usually with a percentage deductible such as 10% to 20% of the dwelling limit.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is flood covered?<svg class="ico chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Not under standard homeowner policies. Flood from rising water requires separate flood insurance, such as through the National Flood Insurance Program.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="faq-4">Working with us</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Are you a public adjuster or a law firm?<svg class="ico chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Neither. We provide appraisal, estimate review and umpire services. We don’t negotiate claims outside the appraisal process and we don’t give legal advice.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do you serve the whole state?<svg class="ico chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes. We work in all 39 Washington counties, from Puget Sound and the coast to the Columbia Basin and the Inland Northwest.</p></div></details><details><summary>What should I send for a first review?<svg class="ico chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>The insurer’s full estimate, your declarations page, any contractor estimate, photos and the most important letters or emails from the insurer.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can I hire you after the insurer has already demanded appraisal?<svg class="ico chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes. We can step in as your appraiser at any point after a demand, as long as the policy’s deadlines are met.</p></div></details></div></article><aside class="side"><div class="side-in"><div class="side-box call-box"><h2 class="box-t">Talk it through, free</h2><p>Tell us what happened and what the insurer offered. We’ll tell you honestly whether appraisal fits.</p><a class="call-num" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-y full" href="/talk-to-an-appraiser/">Send claim details</a></div><div class="side-box"><h2 class="box-t">Resources</h2><ul class="side-links"><li><a href="/how-insurance-appraisal-works/">How appraisal works</a></li><li><a href="/claim-appraisal-faq/" aria-current="page">Appraisal FAQ</a></li><li><a href="/claim-appraisal-glossary/">Claim glossary</a></li><li><a href="/claim-resources/">Claim resources</a></li></ul></div></div></aside></div><section class="cta-strip"><div class="wrap cta-strip-in rv"><div><h2>Didn’t see your question?</h2><p>Call or email and we’ll talk it through.</p></div><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-y" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line light" href="/talk-to-an-appraiser/">Email the estimate</a></div></div></section>
